      "title": "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ce9b49d0-8dd6-52ca-8074-56ed9a3dd832",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=e^{- x y}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/outputs-1/17592186172040/plots/52880ccd3c106c02541bbe76f3b639806d4d37b31730761b8f56fc1496447440.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/outputs-1/17592186172040/plots/06f02d1d1da9e3080d65f2632f766c7ecd3ccc94ca37836194dbe6ed6c0e9fa8.png)",
