  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186172040,
      "id": "a2334101-5266-5853-aedf-04c85746b5cc",
      "title": "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a6e0d5fd-7190-5370-a282-6c84678c8c69",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 x^{2} y?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/outputs-1/17592186172040/plots/9c1beb687736a74c50ed5996c8474cac49c46bf88d308f3e20d2bbbdad8f5e24.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/outputs-1/17592186172040/plots/569d99183bc1653aa9bb10062043e49392611a5b708463dd250d3a084a864e0d.png)",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
